  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nThe current specification defines that when `contenteditable=plaintext-only` is set, users can edit text content. However, it doesn't specify whether non-text nodes (such as images) within such elements can be deleted through user interaction.\n\nThe reason I'm raising this question:\nI'm developing a rich text editor and planning to implement it using `contenteditable=plaintext-only`. My design approach is to let users directly handle both text editing and non-text node deletion through native input, while I'll programmatically handle the insertion and movement of non-text nodes.\n\nThe current problem is:\nI'm unsure whether all browsers support deletion of non-text elements in `contenteditable=plaintext-only` mode. While Chrome does allow this, since the spec doesn't explicitly require supporting non-text element deletion, I can't rely on interoperability. I recommend the spec clearly state that in `plaintext-only` mode, non-text elements should be deletable through direct user input.",
